4139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761083 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=4\\sqrt{x}+x$ at the point with $x$-coordinate $4$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values." 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761083 1072 #uuid "33c230ad-4f63-530c-a4ad-bb1b06cb3ba9" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761083 1074 "First find the point on the curve.\n\n$$\nf(4)=4\\sqrt{(4)}+(4)=12.\n$$\n\nThe normal passes through $(4,12)$.\n\nRewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=4 x^{\\frac{1}{2}} + x, \\\\f'(x)&=1 + \\frac{2}{x^{\\frac{1}{2}}}=1 + \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(4)&=1 + \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(4)}}=2, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(2)}=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(4,12)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(12)&=- \\frac{1}{2}(x-(4)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{2} + 14.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=- \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+(14), \\\\y&=14.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(0,14)$." 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761081 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761104 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761084 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=-2\\sqrt{x}+x$ at the point with $x$-coordinate $4$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values." 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761084 1072 #uuid "34365ec9-a5ed-5c26-9151-b702279b6579" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761084 1074 "First find the point on the curve.\n\n$$\nf(4)=-2\\sqrt{(4)}+(4)=0.\n$$\n\nThe 