1095 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761095 1077 "field-2" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761095 1078 #edb/ref 17592186761091 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761095 1079 #uuid "9e5f5481-423d-5887-84a8-a268232322f6" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761094 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761095 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761096 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=2\\sqrt{x}+3x$ at the point with $x$-coordinate $1$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values." 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761096 1072 #uuid "a04bfa45-58bc-50f8-86ea-6c7b8174dfef" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761096 1074 "First find the point on the curve.\n\n$$\nf(1)=2\\sqrt{(1)}+3(1)=5.\n$$\n\nThe normal passes through $(1,5)$.\n\nRewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=2 x^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 3 x, \\\\f'(x)&=3 + \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{1}{2}}}=3 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(1)&=3 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1)}}=4, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(4)}=- \\frac{1}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,5)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(5)&=- \\frac{1}{4}(x-(1)), \\\\y&=- \\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{21}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=- \\frac{1}{4}x+\\left(\\frac{21}{4}\\right), \\\\x&=21.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(21,0)$." 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761093 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761103 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761097 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=-4\\sqrt{x}+3x+2$ at the point with $x$-coordinate $1$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values." 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761097 1072 #uuid "cdd2a20d-8d5c-5e49-82a4-60608d28d3c9" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761097 1074 "First find the point on the curve.\n\n$$\nf(1)=-4\\sqrt{(1)}+3(1)+2=1.\n$$\n\nThe normal passes through $(1,1)$.\n\nRewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=- 4 x^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 3 x + 2, \\\\f'(x)&=3 - \\frac{2}{x^{\\frac{1}{2}}}=3 - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(1)&=3 - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(1)}}=1, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(1)}=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,1)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(1)&=-1(x-(1)), \\\\y&=- x + 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=-1x+(2), \\\\x&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $(2,0)$." 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761098 1080 "0" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761098 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761098 1083 #uuid "ce7d73fb-48b9-52e0-8db5-ee3ec33734a3" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761099 1080 "6" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761099 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761099 1083 #uuid "d36b435c-29df-52c3-9a8c-07c23f95d5a4" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761100 1080 "21" 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761100 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547579 17592186576411 true] [17592186761100 1083 #uuid "d5fe3843-2666-5a23-